+## 2026-11-17 — `-fno-optimize-sibling-calls` honoured; orphaned marker block behind a boot panic
+
+Booting the 6.18.63 VM kernel built entirely by lccc (1363 TUs, 0 compile
+errors, bzImage 4854784 B) panicked at t=0.87 s in `addrconf_verify_rtnl+0x118`,
+dereferencing `.rodata` — the string `"tx_packets"` — with `net` = `&rtnl_mutex`.
+The chain closed with no free parameters: the kernel passes
+`-fno-optimize-sibling-calls` in every TU, lccc tolerated the spelling and
+converted anyway, and in `addrconf_verify_work` the conversion path lost the
+`container_of` and the argument setup for the *preceding* call, so
+`addrconf_verify_rtnl` received whatever `%rdi` held when `rtnl_lock()`
+returned. gcc-16 performs the same sibling call and still materialises the
+argument, so the conversion is not the problem.
+
+Fixed the option, not the conversion: `-f[no-]optimize-sibling-calls` parsed
+above the `-f` fall-through, carried as `Driver::no_sibling_calls`, published in
+`Driver::run()` via `generation::set_sibling_calls_requested`, and both halves —
+the `ret_dup` IR rewrite and the emitters — now gate on the single
+`generation::sibcall_conversion_enabled()` that already merged `CCC_NO_SIBCALL`,
+so option and kill switch cannot drift. Ledgered `Implemented` with
+`probe-no-sibling-calls`, asserted in both directions (baseline must really
+contain the tail jump) and measured per driver through `SIBCALL_SHAPES`, because
+the withdrawal reads `jmp h@PLT`/`call h@PLT` on x86-64, `b h`/`bl h` on AArch64,
+`tail h`/`call h` on RISC-V, and nothing at all on i686, where the honest pin is
+that the flag changes no bytes. The spelling joined `PRIORITY_SET` so the row
+cannot be dropped again; `check_sibcall_shapes.sh` now pins option ≡ kill switch
+byte-for-byte on all four targets, last-spelling-wins ordering, the kill switch
+still overriding the positive spelling, and runtime parity.
+
+Diagnosed but deliberately NOT fixed: the underlying miscompile, which affects
+default `-O1`/`-O2` userspace, not just kernels. A converted sibling call
+re-publishes its marker block after the `jmp` (`returns.rs` takes
+`sibcall_marker_lines` with `mem::take`) but leaves the block emitted at the
+original call site behind. `liveness.rs::call_markers()` scans
+`n+1..n+CALL_MARKER_WINDOW` (6), breaks at a label or a real instruction, and
+assigns `m.gp_args = v.min(6)` on *every* `# LCCC_CALL_ARGS` it sees — last one
+wins. The orphan sits at `n+3`, the epilogue `popq` that would break the scan is
+at `n+5`, so a 1-argument call is read as 0-argument, `%rdi` becomes provably
+dead, `eliminate_dead_pure_writes` deletes the argument setup and
+`eliminate_dead_flag_writes` then deletes the now-readerless `subq`. Bisection:
+`CCC_PEEPHOLE_SKIP=dead_pure_writes` is correct, `dead_flag_writes` alone is not,
+`CCC_DISABLE_PASSES=ret_dup` does not help (the IR rewrite is innocent), `-O1`
+miscompiles too, and `CCC_NO_PEEPHOLE=1` is correct *with the sibling call still
+present*. `call_markers`' own comment records the same class of bug fixed by
+breaking at labels (gcc.c-torture 981130-1, 980526-2); this shape has no label,
+only the epilogue. Prefer retiring the orphan at the conversion site (one block
+per call site is the invariant the whole marker protocol rests on) over teaching
+the reader first-marker-wins, which touches every peephole that consults call
+liveness. Minimal repro: a derived pointer in a callee-saved register, an
+argument-free call, a call consuming the pointer, and a final void call eligible
+for conversion — `struct net{long pad[274]; struct work chk;}` with
+`entry(w){n=(struct net*)((char*)w-offsetof(struct net,chk)); lock_it();
+consume(n); unlock_it();}`.
+
+Incidental: tolerance is never free, because it answers the kernel's `cc-option`
+probes — lccc tolerating `--param=allow-store-data-races=0` put it on every
+command line, and gcc-16 *rejects* it (GCC 16 replaced it with
+`--allow-store-data-races`). `struct net` layout diverges from gcc-16 by 64 bytes
+(`ipv6.addr_chk_work` at 0x890 vs 0x8d0); harmless for a single-compiler build
+whose `asm-offsets.h` comes from the same compiler, but worth its own look, and
+`-fstrict-flex-arrays=3` is another tolerated-and-ignored spelling. The pre-fix
+build carried 22 objtool warnings where 6.18.52's full VM rebuild was clean: 9
+`unreachable instruction` + 4 `missing __noreturn` are tail-call symptoms, and 9
+UACCESS/alternatives warnings (`stac+0xb: return with UACCESS enabled`,
+`.altinstr_replacement: redundant UACCESS disable`) are a separate class about
+how lccc lowers STAC/CLAC.
+
+Same session, after the fix was measured end to end.
+
+The kernel now boots: `qemu_boot_test.sh` rc=0, ALL CHECKS PASSED (banner,
+SCHED_BORE + its dmesg banner, SCHED_CACHE, CACHE_HOT_BUDDY, HZ, guest config
+equals build config, CACHY, BBRv3 compiled in and listed, PREEMPT, SMP, BORE
+score per task, 2 CPUs online, both serial sentinels verbatim, validation ran to
+completion) — against a build that panicked at t=0.87 s before. bzImage
+4850688 B `5d24502d…`, vmlinux text 10465920 → 10469688 (+3768 B, the tail
+`jmp`s becoming `call` + epilogue, i.e. the cost of honouring the option, in the
+expected direction), ISA scan PASS, setup `_end` unchanged at 0x72e0.
+
+The objtool census was re-measured and **corrected**: 22 → 15, and only **7** of
+the 22 were sibling-call symptoms, not 13. All 7 `unreachable instruction`
+warnings in acpica/{dscontrol,tbdata}, base/dd, 8250/8250_port,
+net/core/dev_ioctl, net/ipv6/{mcast,route} are gone. The 4 `missing __noreturn`
+and 2 `unreachable instruction` that survive are a **distinct lccc-only defect**:
+dead blocks after control-flow terminators. gcc-16 with the same recorded flags
+on the same sources is objtool-clean for all five objects. The wording of the
+noreturn message misleads — `rest_init` *is* in `noreturns.h:43`;
+`check.c:4607` fires when an unvisited instruction follows a `dead_end` call and
+names the callee, so the complaint is about lccc emitting a **full epilogue
+after a call to a `__noreturn` function** (`call rest_init; lea -0x8(%rbp),%rsp;
+pop %rbx; pop %rbp; ret`), where gcc ends the function at the call. The two
+`unreachable instruction` sites are the same thing with a different terminator:
+`rcu_note_context_switch` has a block after an unconditional `jmp` that nothing
+branches to, `compute_live_registers` has a block after a resolved switch jump
+table that is not among its targets. So noreturn-ness is not propagated into
+control flow at all, which is both a size leak and an ORC-metadata leak. The 9
+UACCESS/alternatives warnings are unchanged and remain their own class.
+Measurement traps worth keeping: a naive whole-tree objtool scan reports 121 and
+is meaningless (host objects built with `HOSTCC=gcc` that Kbuild never objtools,
+plus `vmlinux.o`, vDSO, decompressor, realmode, plus 25
+`.static_call_sites`-already-present re-run artefacts); the comparable scope is
+exactly the objects whose `.cmd` file contains an objtool invocation, which is
+1357. objtool's warning count *is* idempotent (2/2/2 on a known object), so
+re-running on in-tree objects is safe.
+
+Also fixed on the way, in `scripts/kernel_tool_identity.sh`: the linker-only
+purge globbed `arch/x86/boot/compressed/vmlinux*`, and in the pristine tree the
+only match besides link products is the **checked-in source `vmlinux.lds.S`**.
+The loss does not fail at purge time; it fails the next build with
+`No rule to make target 'arch/x86/boot/compressed/vmlinux.lds'` after all 1335
+translation units have already been rebuilt — the worst moment to discover a
+deleted source file. The glob is now a sweep that skips source spellings, and
+`check_kernel_tool_identity.sh` seeds `compressed/vmlinux`,
+`compressed/vmlinux.lds` and the checked-in `vmlinux.lds.S`, asserting the
+source survives; it previously seeded only `vmlinux.bin`, which is exactly why
+the defect was invisible. Verified by negative control: restoring the bare glob
+fails the gate, the fix passes it.
+
+The boot gate's emulator was the last unpinned tool in the chain:
+`qemu_boot_test.sh` ran whatever `qemu-system-x86_64` happened to be on `PATH`
+(10.0.13 on this host), yet TCG's instruction decoding, its CPU-model defaults
+and its firmware loading all differ between releases, so the version is part of
+the result and not of the environment. The tag hash cache cannot supply one —
+`harness-cache-3` carries QEMU payloads for **user** mode only
+(`qemu-{aarch64,arm,i386,riscv64}.xz`, pinned 11.1.3), and a `main`-branch patch
+must not add or relabel cache blobs, which live inside orphan tags (see
+[`../FOLLOWUP-2026-10-27-tag-hash-cache-refresh.md`](../FOLLOWUP-2026-10-27-tag-hash-cache-refresh.md)
+§1). So `scripts/ensure_qemu_system.sh` now provisions the *same* 11.1.3 release
+that `ensure_qemu_user.sh` pins, by the *same* mechanism: Debian sid `.deb`s
+pinned by content (SHA-256 from the signed `binary-amd64/Packages.xz` index,
+SHA-1 of the verified bytes as the `snapshot.debian.org` fallback key), eight of
+them — `qemu-system-{x86,common,data}`, `seabios` 1.17.0-1, `ipxe-qemu`
+2.0.0+dfsg-5, plus `libc6`/`libgcc-s1`/`libslirp0`.
+
+glibc has to be part of the pin, which is the non-obvious half. sid's 11.1.3
+requires `GLIBC_2.42`, `GLIBC_2.43` and `SLIRP_4.9`; this host is Debian 13.7
+with glibc 2.41-12+deb13u4, so the binary does not start at all. Rather than
+fall back to the host's older emulator, the prefix carries the glibc the binary
+was built against and `bin/qemu-system-x86_64` is a wrapper that `exec`s through
+the prefix's own `ld-linux-x86-64.so.2` with the prefix's libraries first and
+the host's as the fallback for every version the host already satisfies. No
+root, no system change, nothing written outside the prefix.
+
+Measurement trap worth keeping: **`--version` is not an acceptance test.** The
+first assembled prefix reported `QEMU emulator version 11.1.3` correctly and
+still could not boot — the generated wrapper had baked in the absolute path of
+the staging directory it was assembled in, and the `mv` into place orphaned it
+(`exec: /tmp/lccc-qemu-system.*/prefix/usr/lib64/../lib/…/ld-linux-x86-64.so.2:
+not found`). The check passed because it ran against staging *before* the move.
+Acceptance now requires three things — the pinned version as a whole token
+(rejects both the host's 10.0.13 and a lookalike `11.1.30`), `-machine help`
+listing a `pc` machine and `-cpu help` offering `qemu64` (both ~0.1 s, and they
+prove the loader bootstrap resolves, since `--version` exits before machine
+init), and the four firmware files — and it re-runs in the **final** location,
+not only in staging. The wrapper locates everything from its own position, so
+the prefix survives relocation. `--self-test` pins six rejection cases including
+a `noboot` lookalike that answers `--version` and fails to start.
+
+Layout detail that makes the firmware resolve, and the reason the prefix cannot
+just mirror the dpkg tree: no single package satisfies a firmware directory.
+`bios-256k.bin` comes from `seabios`, `linuxboot_dma.bin` and `kvmvapic.bin`
+from `qemu-system-data`, `efi-e1000.rom` from `ipxe-qemu`. The three QEMU
+consumers in this tree then disagree about what to do with that split —
+`scripts/qemu_boot_test.sh` resolves the four files individually and symlinks a
+throwaway union under `$WORK`; `scripts/kernel_isolate.sh` requires ONE directory
+holding all four and exits 1 with "no firmware union dir" otherwise;
+`scripts/qemu_qmp_probe.py` auto-detects a `share/qemu` next to the binary
+holding the full union. So the prefix ships `share/qemu` as a real directory
+containing a symlink union of both dpkg firmware dirs (60 entries measured: 47
+from qemu-system-data/ipxe-qemu plus 13 seabios-only names), with **relative**
+targets so the union survives the prefix moving. qemu's own files win over
+seabios's same-named ones: they are the copies 11.1.3 was built and tested
+against, and a SeaBIOS `vgabios-*` from another release must not shadow them;
+seabios only fills gaps. That single directory satisfies all three consumers
+with no environment variable and takes `qemu_boot_test.sh`'s "prefer a single
+self-contained directory" fast path. Relocating the whole installed prefix to
+`/tmp` and re-running it — version banner, 37 `pc` machines, all four firmware
+files — is the check that proves both the relative union and that the wrapper
+bakes in no absolute path. Use it as
+`QEMU=$(scripts/ensure_qemu_system.sh --print-bin) scripts/qemu_boot_test.sh`,
+or `eval "$(scripts/ensure_qemu_system.sh --print-env)"` to also export
+`QEMU_DATA_DIR`/`QEMU_ROM_DIR`/`QEMU_BIOS_DIR`.
+
+### DWARF alignment attribute: the encoding, and what D6/D7 bought
+
+`DW_AT_alignment` is **0x88**. **0x21 is `DW_AT_is_optional`**, and writing 0x21
+fails *silently in a way that looks like success*: the abbreviation table stays
+valid, the value is emitted in the right place, readelf decodes the record's
+third attribute as `DW_AT_is_optional : 64`, and pahole simply reports no forced
+alignments. Confirm a producer's attribute code by parsing its **decompressed**
+`.debug_abbrev` (`objcopy --decompress-debug-sections`, then walk the ULEB pairs)
+— `readelf -x` shows the compressed bytes and cannot be used, and gcc's default
+here is `-gz` (its `.debug_abbrev` starts with the zlib magic `78 9c`).
+
+With that fixed and D6 (zero-length arrays as `DW_AT_count`, not
+`DW_AT_upper_bound`) in place, pahole's report for `struct netns_ipv4` on
+`net/ipv6/addrconf.o -gdwarf-4` is character-for-character identical to gcc-16's:
+`size: 896, cachelines: 14, members: 163`, `sum members: 780, holes: 15, sum
+holes: 100`, `padding: 16`, `forced alignments: 4, forced holes: 2, sum forced
+holes: 64`, closing `} __attribute__((__aligned__(64)));`. Before, it reported
+`char x[1]` for each `__cacheline_group_*` marker, `sum members: 788` and
+`BRAIN FART ALERT! 896 != 788 + 98`.
+
+Residual DWARF gaps, measured on that same TU (lccc vs gcc-16): 21 151 vs 24 441
+DIEs (0.865×) — lccc emits less debug information; lccc does not compress debug
+sections while gcc's default `-gz` does, so a naive object-size comparison
+*inverts* the truth (610 KB uncompressed vs 1 410 KB compressed); and
+`DW_AT_alignment` scope differs in **both** directions — on a small probe
+(`#pragma pack`, natural/lower/equal/raised field alignment, alignment via
+typedef, nested aligned struct, aligned global and array) lccc emits 8 and gcc 11,
+the 3 missing being exactly the typedef and the two variable DIEs, while on the
+kernel TU lccc emits *more* on records and members (576 vs 435), so its
+user-alignment predicate is broader than gcc's `TYPE_USER_ALIGN`/`DECL_USER_ALIGN`
+for kernel shapes the probe does not contain.
+
+### objtool census 22 → 15 → 4, and the reachability trap
+
+`scripts/kernel_objtool_census.sh` now makes the census reproducible: it scopes to
+`.cmd` files carrying the kernel-object spelling `; ./tools/objtool/objtool …`
+(1350 objects), measures the 7 further `arch/x86/boot/startup`,
+`arch/x86/entry/vdso` and `arch/x86/realmode` objects separately (0 warnings), and
+totals 1357 — the same scope as the earlier 15-warning measurement, so 15 → 4 is
+like-for-like. It also counts objects objtool *skipped* as already processed
+(`file already has .static_call_sites section, skipping`), which report no
+warnings at all and would silently undercount: 24 here, none of them a warning
+object.
+
+The 8 class-B UACCESS/alternatives warnings are gone with D3, and 3 of the 6
+class-A2 warnings with the noreturn-definitions fix. Of the 4 that remain, two
+(`kernel/rcu/tree.o: rcu_note_context_switch+0x4d5` and
+`kernel/bpf/verifier.o: compute_live_registers+0x12d`) are the same defect: an
+emitted block with **no incoming edge**. In the rcu case the orphaned block is a
+complete `WARN_ONCE` body (`mov $<.rodata ptr>,%rax; mov %rax,-0x30(%rbp); ud2;
+jmp <join>`) that nothing branches to, so that warning can never fire. The IR has
+exactly 11 blocks containing the WARN inline asm with one predecessor edge each
+and the object has 11 `ud2` sites, so it is a lost edge, not duplication. It
+survives `CCC_NO_PEEPHOLE=1` (object grows 157 768 → 166 560 B, orphan unchanged)
+and `CCC_NO_SIBCALL=1` (byte-identical), so it is produced **before** the peephole
+pipeline; a minimal C repro of the same source shape does not reproduce it in
+either compiler, so the trigger is the surrounding CFG (66 IR blocks including a
+`Switch`).
+
+**Measurement trap.** Reachability must account for fallthrough: an instruction
+after a *conditional* jump is reachable. Counting only explicit branch targets
+reports 10 of those 11 `ud2` blocks as orphaned; the correct answer is 1.
+
+The third (`io_uring/io-wq.o: io_wq_worker+0x4eb: io_worker_exit() missing
+__noreturn`) is a **missed inlining opportunity**, not an annotation gap.
+`io_worker_exit` is a local static with exactly one call site; converting the call
+to a tail jump is impossible because `io_wq_worker` returns `void *`/NULL while
+the callee returns `void`, so inlining is the only transformation that removes the
+call. objtool's dead-end inference is correct and its `missing __noreturn` wording
+is generic. The fourth (`lib/iov_iter.o:
+copy_compat_iovec_from_user+0x10b: return with UACCESS enabled`) is not
+root-caused; D3 removed its 8 siblings so it is not the macro-shadowing defect.
+
+### Performance, measured with the sanctioned harness
+
+`scripts/bench_kernels.py` at `-O3`, best-of-7, interleaved arms, driver compiled
+once by a fixed reference compiler, output verified per arm (no correctness
+failure): **geomean 1.222× vs gcc-16** (lccc faster), 0.802× vs clang. Wins:
+`memchr` 43.34× (lccc vectorises, gcc-16 does not; 0.43× the `.text`),
+`namechars` 3.61×. Regressions: `seg_gs_copy` 0.53×, `strcmp_signed` 0.70×,
+`varint` 0.71×, `sqlite_varint_scan` 0.78×, `bn_mul128` 0.86×, `base64_decode`
+0.89×. Total `.text` across the 22 kernels is 0.94× gcc's, and size does not
+predict speed in either direction (`map64_sub` 1.62× the size but 1.33× faster;
+`strcmp_signed` 0.40× the size but 0.70× the speed), so both columns have to be
+reported together.
+
+`seg_gs_copy` is root-caused: in `seg_copy_pass` lccc emits **132 instructions
+against gcc-16's 14** — 43 `movdqu` where gcc uses 4 `movdqa` + 4 `movaps`
+despite both arrays being `__attribute__((aligned(32)))`, **32 scalar `movq`**
+moving 32-byte aggregates 8 bytes at a time, 28 `leaq`, and a loop body that
+opens by spilling index-derived values to fixed stack slots on every trip
+(`leaq 3(%r14),%rax; movq %rax,48(%rsp); leaq 4(%r14),%rax; movq %rax,40(%rsp)`).
+Three separable losses: a live-range/rematerialisation defect (cheapest win),
+alignment not reaching move selection, and incomplete lowering of aggregate
+copies to full-width moves.
+
+The largest absolute gap found is not gcc-relative: `strlen_scan` runs 141.471 ms
+under lccc and 142.020 ms under gcc-16 but **0.085 ms under clang** — a SIMD
+`strlen` idiom neither lccc nor gcc-16 recognises at `-O3`, with lccc's `.text`
+at 0.23× gcc's, so it is a missing idiom rather than a size problem.
+
+### The census is closed, and the seg_gs_copy loop body read in full
+
+`objtool` refuses to re-check an object that already carries `.static_call_sites`
+and such an object reports **no warnings at all**, so a census total is only
+trustworthy if none of the skipped objects would have warned. All 24 skipped
+objects were therefore re-measured properly: each TU recompiled from its own
+`savedcmd_<obj> := …` line into a **fresh** object outside the tree (leaving the
+in-tree object that caused the skip untouched), then objtool run on that. All 24
+compiled rc=0 and produced **0 warnings**, so the census closes at **4 warnings
+over the comparable 1357 objects** with nothing hidden. This also proves the D3 fix
+honestly: `arch/x86/kernel/smp.o` is among the 24 and its 2 earlier `redundant
+UACCESS disable` warnings are genuinely gone rather than masked by the skip.
+
+Reading lccc's emitted loop body for the worst regression (`seg_copy_pass`,
+`.LBB18 → .LBB19`) corrects the headline number and sharpens the diagnosis. lccc
+**unrolls the loop by 4** (128 bytes per trip, pointers advanced with
+`leaq 128(%rbp)`/`128(%r15)`/`128(%r12)`), so the fair comparison is per element:
+**109 instructions per trip / 4 elements = 27.25 against gcc-16's 10**, i.e. 2.7×,
+not the 13× that the raw 132-vs-14 function totals suggest. Inside that body:
+8 `movdqu %xmmN, K(%rsp)` spills against only **3** reloads, so 5 spilled values
+are never reloaded; 10 `movq %rN, K(%rsp)` address-materialisation stores against
+12 loads; **8 stores are dead outright** (4 xmm + 4 GPR, the slot is overwritten
+before anything reloads it); 25 `leaq`; 30 scalar `movq`. Even the loop *counter*
+is round-tripped — the body opens with
+`leaq 3(%r14), %rax; movq %rax, 48(%rsp); leaq 4(%r14), %rax; movq %rax, 40(%rsp)`
+and closes with `movq 40(%rsp), %r14; cmpq $256, %r14; jl .LBB18`, where gcc-16
+does `addq $32, %rax` on an induction variable that never leaves a register.
+
+The 8 dead stores survive because `eliminate_dead_pure_writes`
+(`src/backend/x86/codegen/peephole/passes/dead_writes.rs:227`) filters its
+candidate on `plain_gp_operand(dst)` and `is_writable_family_gpr(fam)`, so it only
+ever considers a **general-purpose register destination**; an xmm destination and
+a memory destination such as `16(%rsp)` are structurally out of scope, and the
+same holds for `eliminate_dead_flag_writes` (`:154`). The deeper difference is
+addressing: gcc-16 folds `bench_seg_src` and `gen_blk` into `symbol+offset(%rax)`
+operands on one induction variable using zero stack slots, while lccc
+re-materialises each address with `leaq` and routes it through a stack slot.
